Optimal Solution - Divide-and-conquer merge of k sorted linked lists achieving O(N log k) time complexity.
                   This is merge sort on linked lists — repeatedly merge pairs until one sorted list remains.

class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {
        int k = lists.length;
        // If no lists
        if (k == 0) return null;
        // Divide and conquer merge
        return partition(0, k - 1, lists);
    }
    // Recursively divide the lists array
    public ListNode partition(int start, int end, ListNode[] lists) {
        // No list
        if (start > end) return null;
        // Only one list
        if (start == end) return lists[start];
        int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
        // Merge left half
        ListNode Lt1 = partition(start, mid, lists);
        // Merge right half
        ListNode Lt2 = partition(mid + 1, end, lists);
        // Merge two sorted lists
        return mergeTwoList(Lt1, Lt2);
    }
    // Merge two sorted linked lists
    public ListNode mergeTwoList(ListNode l1, ListNode l2) {
        if (l1 == null) return l2;
        if (l2 == null) return l1;
        if (l1.val <= l2.val) {
            l1.next = mergeTwoList(l1.next, l2);
            return l1;
        } else {
            l2.next = mergeTwoList(l1, l2.next);
            return l2;
        }
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N log K).
Space Complexity :- O(log K).
